README-KERMIT -- five kermits, and which one to takeKermit moves files over a serial line to another machine, so these run onyour own OS-9 system; os9exec has no serial line to try them on.Take `ckermit' if you have the room: C-Kermit 5A(190), with the wholeprotocol and a command language, at 356K.  Otherwise take `kermit': 26K,no Microware module, and its full source is here.THE FIVE--------  file        bytes  cio  module   source       docs  ------------------------------------------------------------  kermit      26828  no   kermit   SRC/kermit   DOC/kermit  kermit2     21998  no   kermit2  --           DOC/kermit2  kermit3     18148  no   Kermit   --           DOC/kermit3  xkermit     14176  yes  xkermit  SRC/kermit   DOC/kermit  ckermit    356040  yes  wermit   --           DOC/ckermit`xkermit' is `kermit' -- OS-9 Kermit 1.5 -- linked to Microware's `cio'rather than carrying its own stdio, hence half the size.`ckermit' reports itself as:    C-Kermit 5A(190) BETA.14, 24 Jul 94    OS-9 Communications I/O, 5A(009) 9 Dec 93 for OS-9/68000    OS-9 file support, 5A(08) 01 Dec 92 for OS-9/68000    C-Kermit Protocol Module 5A(066), 24 Jul 94TAKE THIS IF------------  ckermit    you want a kermit a modern kermit will talk to without             argument, and a scripting language to drive it.  kermit     you want one small file that works, with its source.  This is             the one to read if you are porting.  kermit2    you want something between the two.  No source.  kermit3    you want the smallest of the plain ones.  xkermit    you already carry `cio' and want `kermit' smaller.MODULE NAMES------------Once a module is resident OS-9 finds it by module name.  Two of thesehave module names that are not their filenames:    kermit3    reports itself as `Kermit'    ckermit    reports itself as `wermit', C-Kermit's own name for its               OS-9 buildOS-9 compares module names without regard to case, so `Kermit' and`kermit' are one name: do not load kermit3 and kermit together.THE TWO FLAGS THAT MATTER-------------------------Neither gives an error when you get it wrong.  l (line)   names the device.  `kermit s /t1 file' sends over the console             and ignores /t1; `kermit sl /t1 file' uses /t1.  i (image)  for binary files.  Not `8', which is 8-bit quoting for a link             that is not 8-bit clean, and corrupts data over one that is.  kermit sil /t1 mymodule      sends a module intactWHAT THEY WANT BESIDES THEMSELVES---------------------------------A serial device.  None reads a configuration file here; `ckermit' reads a`.kermrc' if you give it one.